    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Trends come and go, so you should wear what makes you happy. In fact, the idea of feeling good by what we wear has a name—"
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "dopamine dressing",
            "zh": "多巴胺穿搭"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The term is the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "brainchild",
            "zh": "独创的想法"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of Dawnn Karen. She is the author of the book "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "Dress Your Best Life"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". In the book, she wrote about looking for joy through dress."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Dopamine is the feel-good chemical our bodies make. At the start of the pandemic, Karen started looking for ways to improve dopamine levels."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“Dopamine can be released by doing other things, but what about wearing something crazy or bright colours?” says Karen. She then created “dopamine dressing”. The term quickly became popular."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The dopamine dressing trend is most alive on social media. Fashion "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "influencers",
            "zh": "网红"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " often wear head-to-toe colourful looks. They’re challenging their creativity to include more colours in their wardrobes. In general, the post-pandemic world also sees the public wearing more colours."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "It’s easy to understand why. Bright and eye-catching clothing "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "functions",
            "ref": "v-function"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " like a rainbow after the storm. Other examples can be found in history. Think of the famously "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "flamboyant",
            "zh": "绚丽的"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " clothes of the 1920s after the First World War."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "i1",
        "type": "imageRef",
        "image": "img1"
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Of course, it’s impossible to define exactly which colour we should wear to improve our mood, and dopamine dressing doesn’t suggest that we should all wear bright "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "neons",
            "zh": "亮丽色彩"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " to feel happier. For some, wearing black might bring more happiness than wearing bright colours. This "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "is",
            "ref": "v-be-down-to"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " all "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "down to",
            "ref": "v-be-down-to"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " one’s personal "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "associations",
            "ref": "v-association"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " with a "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "given",
            "ref": "v-given"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " colour."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p8",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "All in all, if you’re looking to encourage feelings of happiness through your clothing, you don’t need to turn to a specific colour. Which colours have you always loved? Just go for them."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p9",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“Add more of your favourite colours to your wardrobe. Think about the colours that make you think of a happier time, a place, or a person,” says Shakaila Forbes-Bell, a fashion "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "psychologist",
            "zh": "心理学家"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". “What do you associate with joy? Go ahead and wear it!”"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
